# SORABBYNGO Security Hardening — OpenBSD-inspired options
#

menu "Sorabbyngo Security Hardening"

config SORABBYNGO_WX_STRICT
	bool "Strict W^X enforcement (OpenBSD-inspired)"
	depends on MMU
	default y
	help
	  Reject any mmap(2) or mprotect(2) call that would result in a
	  page being simultaneously writable and executable. Processes
	  without CAP_SYS_ADMIN get EACCES.

	  Matches OpenBSD's default W^X policy. Recommended for all
	  Sorabbyngo OS surfaces. JIT compilers must drop PROT_WRITE
	  before adding PROT_EXEC.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_PLEDGE
	bool "Pledge syscall promise sets (OpenBSD pledge(2) adaptation)"
	depends on SECCOMP_FILTER
	default y
	help
	  Allows processes to voluntarily restrict their syscall surface
	  via prctl(PR_SET_SORABBYNGO_PLEDGE). Once pledged, the promise
	  set can only be narrowed. Violations send SIGKILL.

	  Byngox agents, Chameha, and CML use this at startup to drop
	  unneeded capabilities before entering their main loop.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_UNVEIL
	bool "Unveil filesystem path restriction (OpenBSD unveil(2) adaptation)"
	depends on SORABBYNGO_PLEDGE
	default y
	help
	  Allows processes to restrict their visible filesystem to an
	  explicit set of paths. All other paths return ENOENT, making
	  them invisible rather than forbidden.

	  Chameha uses this to restrict its view to /opt/sorabbyngo/chameha
	  and /proc/sorabbyngo. CML restricts to its log and config dirs.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_SLAB_GUARD
	bool "Slab guard pages and use-after-free detection (OpenBSD malloc hardening)"
	default y
	help
	  Adds guard page insertion and free-poison detection to the kernel
	  slab allocator, inspired by OpenBSD's malloc(3) hardening.

	  Freed chunks are poisoned with 0xDF. Allocation verifies the
	  poison pattern to catch use-after-free writes.

	  Small performance cost (~2-3%) in exchange for heap overflow
	  detection. Recommended for all Sorabbyngo OS surfaces.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_SLAB_GUARD_INTERVAL
	int "Guard page insertion interval (allocations)"
	depends on SORABBYNGO_SLAB_GUARD
	default 64
	range 16 512
	help
	  Insert a guard page after every N slab allocations.
	  Lower values = more protection, higher memory overhead.
	  Default of 64 is a good balance for Byngox agent workloads.

config SORABBYNGO_NET_POLICY
	bool "Default-deny network policy (OpenBSD pf-inspired)"
	depends on NETFILTER && IP_NF_FILTER
	default y
	help
	  Enforces a whitelist-based network policy at the netfilter hook
	  level, inspired by OpenBSD's pf(4) default-deny posture.

	  All traffic not matching an explicit allow rule is dropped.
	  Byngonet NodeID-verified peers are automatically allowed.
	  Loopback traffic is always allowed.

	  Configure via /proc/sorabbyngo/net_policy.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_ANTISPOOF
	bool "Anti-spoofing strict reverse path filtering"
	depends on SORABBYNGO_NET_POLICY
	default y
	help
	  Enable strict reverse-path filtering on all interfaces.
	  Mirrors OpenBSD pf's "antispoof" directive.
	  Drops packets whose source address fails a reverse-path check.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_TCP_HARDENING
	bool "TCP stack hardening (OpenBSD-inspired)"
	depends on INET
	default y
	help
	  Enables several TCP hardening features inspired by OpenBSD:
	    - SipHash-2-4 based ISN generation (vs MD5 in OpenBSD)
	    - Per-connection TCP timestamp randomisation
	    - RST rate limiting (10/sec per source)
	    - Aggressive SYN cookie threshold (32 vs Linux default 1024)

	  Protects Byngonet peer connections and NATS JetStream bus.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_PER_TASK_CANARY
	bool "Per-task stack canary reseeding (OpenBSD-inspired)"
	depends on STACKPROTECTOR_STRONG
	default y
	help
	  Each task gets a unique stack canary derived from get_random_long()
	  mixed with the NodeID. Reseeded at fork() and exec().

	  A canary leak in one process cannot be used to attack another.
	  Matches OpenBSD's per-process canary behaviour.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config SORABBYNGO_KASLR_ENTROPY
	bool "Enhanced kASLR entropy (OpenBSD-inspired TSC jitter mixing)"
	depends on RANDOMIZE_BASE
	default y
	help
	  Mixes additional TSC jitter into the kASLR seed at early boot,
	  and extends mmap/stack ASLR entropy to 28/16 bits respectively.

	  Mirrors OpenBSD's boot-time entropy accumulation from locore.S.

	  If unsure, say Y.

endmenu
